/* HELLFORGE v1.0.0.0 ALPHA — CORE-EXPANSION: REGAS */
// .enx album root — ordered track listing
   File:       album.enx
   Version:    v4 (album root index) — .enx extension
   Status:     CURRENT STANDARD
   
   .enx files are album-level root indexes that define a collection
   of tracks for ordered playback. An album contains metadata and
   a sequence of track references that the HELLFORGE player reads
   and plays in order — like a playlist or album track listing.
   
   The .enx format is designed for:
   - Full album/EP playback
   - Concert setlists
   - Multi-movement works
   - Looping background music for games/apps

/* @title "My First HELLFORGE Album" — Album/collection title.
   Displayed by the player when the album is loaded. */
@title "My First HELLFORGE Album"

/* @artist "HELLFORGE User" — Artist/composer name metadata. */
@artist "HELLFORGE User"

/* @year 2026 — Release year for metadata tagging. */
@year 2026

/* **track tracks/song1.e** — Reference to the first track.
   The **track** keyword tells the album index to load and play
   the referenced .e file as a track.
   
   Tracks are played in the order they appear.
   Each track can have its own tempo, key, and structure.
   The player pauses between tracks (configurable gap). */
**track tracks/song1.e**

/* A second track (placeholder for future songs).
   In a full album, you would list all tracks here in sequence.
   The player automatically advances to the next track when
   the current track finishes.

   Example of additional tracks (uncomment when files exist):
   **track tracks/song2.e**
   **track tracks/song3.e**
   **track tracks/song4.e**
*/

/* Album playback options (configurable per .enx):
   - Tracks play sequentially by default.
   - Loop mode: add @loop at the album level to repeat endlessly.
   - Shuffle mode: add @shuffle to randomize track order on each play.
   - Crossfade: add @crossfade 3 to crossfade between tracks (seconds). */

// Run from project root: py ep.py compile samples/enx/album.enx
